{"authored" [{"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked\n\nAssume independent trials with a constant success probability strictly between $0$ and $1$ for each experiment. Replace and mix each crayon before the next draw." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/review.json" "file_sha256" "9449edc53ad96e2995ca134acb595b584c864358117ccb160a847bc3e0d8ed64" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "9223a2dadc2481fdde95e0e44e30362d01d559306024e1a6b2a64a15d5aefdd4" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/verification.json" "basis" 2431 "rationale" "The actual Correct grade for choice e and the revealed source solution establish author intent. The literal source omitted replacement, independence, and constant probability. The clarification supplies those model assumptions; I and III are geometric with different supports and II is a total count. No wrong source key is alleged."} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution models waiting for the first success in independent Bernoulli trials with constant success probability.\n\n- I counts file checks through the first infected file. Under the stated model, $P(T=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p$ for $k=1,2,\\ldots$.\n- II counts the total infected files in a computer; it does not count trials until the first success.\n- III counts nonblue draws before the first blue crayon. Replacement keeps the probability of blue constant. Under the stated model, $P(F=k)=(1-q)^kq$ for $k=0,1,2,\\ldots$.\n\nI and III are geometric, using the trials and failures conventions respectively. The correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\nThe original source omits the independence and constant-probability assumptions and does not specify crayon replacement. Without replacement, III generally is not geometric; the study wording makes the intended model explicit." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/review.json" "file_sha256" "9449edc53ad96e2995ca134acb595b584c864358117ccb160a847bc3e0d8ed64" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "9223a2dadc2481fdde95e0e44e30362d01d559306024e1a6b2a64a15d5aefdd4" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/verification.json" "basis" 2431 "rationale" "The actual Correct grade for choice e and the revealed source solution establish author intent. The literal source omitted replacement, independence, and constant probability. The clarification supplies those model assumptions; I and III are geometric with different supports and II is a total count. No wrong source key is alleged."} {"question" "q-130486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/review.json" "file_sha256" "9449edc53ad96e2995ca134acb595b584c864358117ccb160a847bc3e0d8ed64" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "9223a2dadc2481fdde95e0e44e30362d01d559306024e1a6b2a64a15d5aefdd4" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-130486/verification.json" "basis" 2431 "rationale" "The actual Correct grade for choice e and the revealed source solution establish author intent. The literal source omitted replacement, independence, and constant probability. The clarification supplies those model assumptions; I and III are geometric with different supports and II is a total count. No wrong source key is alleged."} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c512ca3479f906dbe40920997aa08c178a049eeaa6d7b84fd78398d9a30adde" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Reviewing a file is a binomial experiment, and the number of files reviewed before the first infected file is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Finding an infected file is a binomial experiment, but the number of infected files in a computer cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c512ca3479f906dbe40920997aa08c178a049eeaa6d7b84fd78398d9a30adde" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "484d02501250207db859bd958253508a50fce1732d70893ec895156fcc92a1b2" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c512ca3479f906dbe40920997aa08c178a049eeaa6d7b84fd78398d9a30adde" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-130486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069917/state.json" "file_sha256" "4c512ca3479f906dbe40920997aa08c178a049eeaa6d7b84fd78398d9a30adde" "basis" 2429 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-captur